{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"30877432d1026706","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What is a subnet?**|What is a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":0,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d7e805da846a32c3","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is a smaller network inside a `VPC`.  \nYou divide a VPC into subnets to organize your AWS resources.  \nFor example, your VPC may have this CIDR block:\n```text\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen you can divide it into smaller subnets:\n```text\n10.0.1.0/24\n10.0.2.0/24\n10.0.3.0/24\n```\nThe VPC is the big network.  \nThe subnets are smaller networks inside it.  \n**Do subnets need to be inside the VPC CIDR block?**  \nYes.  \nSubnets must be inside the VPC CIDR block.  \nFor example, if your VPC is:\n```text\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen these subnets are valid:\n```text\n10.0.1.0/24\n10.0.2.0/24\n10.0.3.0/24\n```\nBecause they are inside the `10.0.0.0/16` range.  \n**Can you create a subnet outside the VPC range?**  \nNo.  \nYou cannot create a subnet outside the VPC CIDR block.  \nFor example, if your VPC is:\n```text\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen this subnet is not valid:\n```text\n192.168.1.0/24\n```\nBecause `192.168.1.0/24` is outside the VPC range.  ","x":350,"y":0,"width":600,"height":1085},
		{"id":"73c8eb5bb682575e","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**How many IP addresses does AWS reserve in a subnet?**|How many IP addresses does AWS reserve in a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":1145,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"c87a171ac826a6fc","type":"text","text":"AWS reserves `5 IP addresses` in every subnet.  \nThese IP addresses are used by AWS for internal networking.  \nSo you cannot use all IP addresses in a subnet.  \n**Example with `/28` subnet**  \nA `/28` subnet has:\n```text\n16 total IP addresses\n```\nBut AWS reserves:\n```text\n5 IP addresses\n```\nSo you can only use:\n```text\n11 IP addresses\n```\nFormula:\n```text\n16 total IP addresses - 5 reserved IP addresses = 11 usable IP addresses\n```","x":350,"y":1145,"width":600,"height":555},
		{"id":"5d2cce8038a39d5e","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**Why can you not use all IP addresses in a subnet?**|Why can you not use all IP addresses in a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":1760,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"0853964b50af03b9","type":"text","text":"You cannot use all IP addresses because AWS keeps some addresses for internal networking.  \nAWS uses them for things like:\n```text\nNetwork address\nVPC router\nDNS\nFuture AWS use\nBroadcast address\n```\nSo when planning subnet size, always remember that AWS reserves 5 IP addresses.  ","x":350,"y":1760,"width":600,"height":330},
		{"id":"cbee3772a0770217","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What decides if a subnet is public or private?**|What decides if a subnet is public or private?]]","x":-100,"y":2130,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"21a278f64f7fd633","type":"text","text":"A subnet type is decided by routing.  \nA subnet becomes public or private because of its `route table`.  \nThe subnet itself is not automatically public or private.  \nThe route table decides what kind of network access the subnet has.  ","x":350,"y":2130,"width":600,"height":135},
		{"id":"d58e72e310275dff","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What is a public subnet?**|What is a public subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":2325,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"6c15c0c8e9df4696","type":"text","text":"A `public subnet` is a subnet that has a route to an `Internet Gateway`.  \nFor example:\n```text\nPublic subnet\nRoute table\n0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway\n```\nThis means traffic from the subnet can go to the internet.  \nIt also means internet-facing resources can be placed in this subnet.  ","x":350,"y":2325,"width":600,"height":275},
		{"id":"12da86a78c49ea20","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What does `0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway` mean?**|What does `0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway` mean?]]","x":-100,"y":2660,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"e32684b27b95e909","type":"text","text":"```text\n0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway\n```\n`0.0.0.0/0` means all IPv4 internet traffic.  \nSo this route means:\n```text\nSend internet traffic to the Internet Gateway\n```\nIf a subnet route table has this route, the subnet is usually considered public.  ","x":350,"y":2660,"width":600,"height":270},
		{"id":"d810a485ed03241b","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What resources usually go in a public subnet?**|What resources usually go in a public subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":2990,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"4d419b1b673bd475","type":"text","text":"A public subnet usually contains internet-facing resources.  \nFor example:\n```text\nWeb server\nLoad balancer\nBastion host\n```\nThese resources may need to receive traffic from the internet.  ","x":350,"y":2990,"width":600,"height":250},
		{"id":"b97706ca828bca03","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What is a private subnet?**|What is a private subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":3300,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"85813dbad3c681d0","type":"text","text":"A `private subnet` does not have a direct route to the `Internet Gateway`.  \nFor example:\n```text\nPrivate subnet\nRoute table\nNo route directly to Internet Gateway\n```\nThis means the internet cannot directly reach resources in this subnet.  ","x":350,"y":3300,"width":600,"height":250},
		{"id":"c59c0996daeee6f5","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What resources usually go in a private subnet?**|What resources usually go in a private subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":3610,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"29a279764017f2ed","type":"text","text":"A private subnet usually contains internal resources.  \nFor example:\n```text\nDatabase\nBackend server\nInternal service\n```\nThese resources should not be directly exposed to the public internet.  \n**Can a private subnet access the internet?**  \nYes, but not directly through an Internet Gateway.  \nA private subnet can access the internet through a `NAT Gateway`.  \nExample:\n```text\nPrivate subnet → NAT Gateway → Internet Gateway → Internet\n```\nThis allows private resources to download updates or call external APIs.  \nBut the internet cannot directly connect back to those private resources.  ","x":350,"y":3610,"width":600,"height":480},
		{"id":"4eaede5fe878f78e","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What is an Availability Zone?**|What is an Availability Zone?]]","x":-100,"y":4150,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"2978aa2447c462dd","type":"text","text":"`Availability Zone`, or `AZ`, is a separate data center area inside an AWS Region.  \nFor example, one AWS Region can have multiple AZs:\n```text\nap-southeast-1a\nap-southeast-1b\nap-southeast-1c\n```\nEach AZ is separated from the others, so a problem in one AZ should not always affect the others.  ","x":350,"y":4150,"width":600,"height":282},
		{"id":"f78df0cac5e40c02","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**Why should you use multiple AZs?**|Why should you use multiple AZs?]]","x":-100,"y":4460,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d4e518ca6eaac8d8","type":"text","text":"You should use multiple AZs so your app can keep running if one AZ has a problem.  \nIf everything is only in one AZ, then your app may stop when that AZ fails.  \nBetter design:\n```text\nPublic subnet in AZ 1\nPublic subnet in AZ 2\nPrivate subnet in AZ 1\nPrivate subnet in AZ 2\n```\nThis gives your app better availability.  \n**Example multi-AZ subnet design**  \nExample VPC:\n```text\nVPC: 10.0.0.0/16\n├── AZ 1\n│   ├── Public subnet: 10.0.1.0/24\n│   └── Private subnet: 10.0.2.0/24\n│\n└── AZ 2\n    ├── Public subnet: 10.0.3.0/24\n    └── Private subnet: 10.0.4.0/24\n```\nThis design spreads resources across two AZs.  \nSo if one AZ has a problem, the other AZ can still run part of the app.  ","x":350,"y":4460,"width":600,"height":695},
		{"id":"36e8b5359309cc62","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/2. Checklist for subnets#**What are AWS resources?**|What are AWS resources?]]","x":-100,"y":5215,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"73931bdb2a0df3db","type":"text","text":"`AWS resources` are services or objects created in AWS.  \nExamples:\n```text\nEC2 instances\nRDS databases\nLambda functions\nLoad balancers\nVPCs\nSubnets\nSecurity groups\n```\nThese are the building blocks of your AWS infrastructure.  \n**Simple idea**  \nA `subnet` is a smaller network inside a VPC.  \nSubnets must be inside the VPC CIDR block.  \nAWS reserves 5 IP addresses in every subnet.  \nA subnet is public or private because of its route table.  \nA public subnet has a route to the Internet Gateway.  \nA private subnet does not have a direct route to the Internet Gateway.  \nUse multiple AZs so your app can survive problems in one AZ.  ","x":350,"y":5215,"width":600,"height":570}
	],
	"edges":[
